WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.542
The null if function is very useful when one specific value should be treated as missing.

00:00:05.542 --> 00:00:08.522
Here we trim spaces from the address.

00:00:08.522 --> 00:00:13.467
If the result is an empty string, the null if function returns null.

00:00:13.467 --> 00:00:16.579
Otherwise, it keeps the trimmed address.

00:00:17.029 --> 00:00:21.136
Suppose one record contains several spaces in the address field.

00:00:21.136 --> 00:00:25.851
Without trimming, the database technically sees a text value.

00:00:25.851 --> 00:00:32.431
After trimming, nothing remains, so we can represent that known blank state as null.

00:00:32.431 --> 00:00:39.461
This is a reasonable transformation only when our rule says blank addresses should be considered missing.

00:00:39.461 --> 00:00:43.491
Compare the original address with the normalized address.

00:00:43.491 --> 00:00:47.321
The point is not that null is always better than a blank.

00:00:47.321 --> 00:00:54.744
The point is that consistent missing-value representation makes later analysis and quality checks much easier.
